course: dictionary
lesson: 8534
title: One word, four meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word saw can mean different things, and the sentence tells you which one. «I saw a bird.» Here saw is the past form of see, so it means I looked at something and noticed it.
explain: «Dad fetched the saw from the shed.» A saw is a tool with a sharp, toothed blade for cutting wood or metal. You can tell because fetched and shed are things you carry and keep in places, and a tool fits that.
explain: To saw is to cut with a saw, so «She will saw the plank in half» is an action. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «I looked at a bird» still makes sense, you have found the right one. An old saying is also called a saw, so read the whole sentence before you decide.
fact: Saw is the past form of see: see, sees, saw, seen, seeing.
fact: A saw is a noun: a tool with a sharp, toothed blade for cutting wood or metal.
fact: To saw is a verb: to cut with a saw.
fact: An old saying is sometimes called a saw.
fact: In maths stories, «saw» often means the children counted things they saw, and a board cut into pieces needs one fewer cut than the number of pieces.
exercise: In «I saw a bird», what does saw mean? | To cut wood with a blade | *Past form of see: I looked and noticed | A tool with a toothed blade | An old saying
exercise: What is a saw? | A tall tree | *A tool with a sharp, toothed blade for cutting | A soft cushion
exercise: A board is sawed into 3 pieces. How many cuts were made? | 3 | 4 | *2 | 1
test: In a rebus, a picture of an eye and a saw can spell which words? | Saw saw | *I saw | Eye see | Sea and saw
test: Which word is the past form of see? | *saw | sees | seeing | seen
exam: Which sentence uses saw as a tool? | «Look, I saw it with my own eyes.» | *«Dad fetched the saw from the shed.» | «The bell rang and we saw the teacher.» | «I saw a bird from the window.»
